gemi
====
A gemini browser, written in C++.

Requirements
============
C++ compiler, make, openssl, ncurses

Installation
============
Edit config.mk to match your local setup if necessary for building (gemi
will be installed into /usr/local/bin by default).

Then run the following command to build and install gemi:
    make install

(the above command may require root, depending on the installation location)

Usage
=====
Open a specific site:
    gemi {url}

(gemini:// is optional)

Open a local gemtext file:
    gemi {path}

Start gemi:
    gemi


Identities
==========
These are specified in:
    $HOME/.gmi/{hostname}/client.crt
    $HOME/.gmi/{hostname}/client.key

Or for a non-standard port (port != 1965):
    $HOME/.gemi/{hostname}:{port}/client.crt
    $HOME/.gemi/{hostname}:{port}/client.key

Example:
    > tree .gemi
    .gemi
    └── bbs.geminispace.org
        ├── client.crt
        └── client.key

(I recommend symlinking ~/.gemi to somewhere you backup regularly)

Example command to generate keys:
    openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 3650   -keyout client.key -out client.crt -subj "/CN=localhost"

Caching
=======
All caching is done in memory with a configurable fifo data structure to limit cached page count. 
There are two seperate instances of the cache object at any given time. One for prefetching and one for history.
Pages fetched with an identity are never cached, and prefetching never sends requests to hosts you'd use an identity for.

    Prefetch caching:

    One of the main reasons this browser feels so much faster than other gemini protocol browsers is because of its optional prefetch caching. 
    This is enabled by default. When you visit a site without an identity, a thread will be created that fetches the first 10 links on the page.
    This enables ~ instantaneous visitation of those links. Prefetch caching is only enabled when you aren't using an identity for a domain.
    Per the gemtext specification, "clients must not automatically make any network connections as part of displaying links", so this mode of
    operation may be construed as non-compliant with the gemtext specification. I don't believe a file format specification should be prescriptive
    about such things. Additionally, if this was a feature in a http-based web browser, I would disable it. I perceive Gemini as being more 
    trustworthy right now and thus am not concerned about having this defaulted on. 

    History caching:

    When you visit a page, the page will be cached after it's loaded. 
    If the page was in the prefetch cache before, it will be upgraded to the history cache. 
    This is because visited pages are more important than prefetched ones. 
    History caching is disabled when using an identity.
